-- Passage rg4-text-structure-p05
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('23a18cf8-f30a-587c-91b7-498afc0af8f8', 'rg4-text-structure-p05', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g4-text-structure'), null,
        '3-5', $q$The Story of the Bicycle$q$, $q$The bicycle has changed a lot over the past two hundred years. Each new design solved a problem with the one before it.

In 1817, an inventor in Germany built a wooden riding machine. It had two wheels but no pedals. Riders pushed against the ground with their feet, like riding a scooter.

About fifty years later, in the 1860s, makers in France added pedals to the front wheel. Now riders could move without touching the ground. However, the ride was so bumpy on stone streets that people called these bikes "boneshakers."

In the 1870s, bikes with a giant front wheel became popular. The huge wheel let riders go faster. Still, sitting up so high made it easy to fall.

Then, in the 1880s, the "safety bicycle" arrived. Its two wheels were the same size, and a chain turned the back wheel. Soon after, air-filled rubber tires made the ride smooth.

Today's bikes look a lot like the safety bicycle. The basic design has lasted more than one hundred years.$q$, 'draft')
